First, we draw a picture. We can see that the shortest path is a straight line from the gecko to the fly. We draw a line from the gecko to the fly and label the points. Let $A$ be the gecko, $B$ be the fly, and $C$ be the point where the gecko and the fly meet the ceiling. Then, we draw a line segment from $C$ to the back wall, which we label $CD$. We can see that $\triangle ACD$ is a right triangle, so we can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find $CD$.
\begin{align*}
CD^2 &= AC^2 - AD^2 \\
&= 12^2 - 10^2 \\
&= 144 - 100 \\
&= 44 \\
CD &= \sqrt{44} \\
CD &= 2\sqrt{11}
\end{align*}
Next, we draw a line segment from $C$ to the front wall, which we label $CE$. We can see that $\triangle BCE$ is also a right triangle, so we can use the Pythagorean Theorem again to find $CE$.
\begin{align*}
CE^2 &= BC^2 - BE^2 \\
&= 10^2 - 8^2 \\
&= 100 - 64 \\
&= 36 \\
CE &= \sqrt{36} \\
CE &= 6
\end{align*}
Finally, we draw a line segment from $C$ to the ceiling, which we label $CF$. We can see that $\triangle CFE$ is also a right triangle, so we can use the Pythagorean Theorem again to find $CF$.
\begin{align*}
CF^2 &= CE^2 - EF^2 \\
&= 6^2 - 1^2 \\
&= 36 - 1 \\
&= 35 \\
CF &= \sqrt{35}
