\|\overrightarrow{AB}\| &= \sqrt{(2)^2 + (7)^2 + (-1)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{4 + 49 + 1} \\
&= \sqrt{54} \\
&= 3\sqrt{6}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\|\overrightarrow{AC}\| &= \sqrt{(-3)^2 + (12)^2 + (-3)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{9 + 144 + 9} \\
&= \sqrt{152} \\
&= 2\sqrt{38}
\end{align*}

Finally, we can find the cosine of the angle $\angle ABC$:
\begin{align*}
\cos(\angle ABC) &= \frac{\overrightarrow{AB} \cdot \overrightarrow{AC}}{\|\overrightarrow{AB}\| \|\overrightarrow{AC}\|} \\
&= \frac{81}{3\sqrt{6} \cdot 2\sqrt{38}} \\
&= \frac{81}{6\sqrt{228}} \\
&= \frac{27}{2\sqrt{228}} \\
&= \frac{27}{2 \cdot 2\sqrt{57}} \\
&= \frac{27}{4\sqrt{57}} \\
&= \frac{27\sqrt{57}}{228} \\
&= \frac{9\sqrt{57}}{76}
\end{align*}

To find the angle in degrees, we take the inverse cosine of $\cos(\angle ABC)$ and multiply by 180 degrees:
\begin{align*}
\angle ABC &= \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{9\sqrt{57}}{76}\right) \\
&\approx 53.13^\circ
\end{align*}

